## 1. What Went Well?

- The team made good progress on the main frontend and backend pages, including authentication, service browsing and booking interfaces. Every User story can be finishied before due day.
- The initial backend domain model and database relationships were established using Spring Boot, JPA and H2.
- Team members communicated their progress and divided the Sprint 1 tasks across frontend, backend and documentation work.
- Git and GitHub were used to share and integrate individual contributions.


---

## 2. What Went Not So Well?

- Frontend and backend integration was started later than expected, so some frontend features still relied on mock data.
- The API contract and backend folder structure were not agreed upon early enough, which led to changes in entity fields, naming and responsibilities.
- Some user stories were too large to complete comfortably within one sprint.
- Some members hard to aglin with team code structure, sometime cannot follow the team progress.
- Team communication are not good enough, some code structure still have some diverfence, and implement at same time lead to crash.
- Some members did not spend enough time on development, and some tasks were started later than planned, which affected the overall progress.
- Testing was not completed thoroughly, and some features did not receive sufficient testing and verification.

---

## 3. Lessons Learned

### What should be improved or done differently in the team's process?

- The team should begin Sprint tasks earlier to allow enough time for development, integration, and testing.
- Progress should be updated continuously instead of being reported only during meetings.
- When tasks or code change, the relevant team members should be informed promptly to reduce waiting time and duplicated work.
- Testing should be performed alongside feature development rather than being left until the end of the Sprint.

### Was there anything unexpected that should be taken into account in future sprints?

- Some tasks took longer than expected because of dependencies between team members’ code.
- Delayed progress updates created unexpected blockers for other team members.
- Changes to JPA entities affected the generated database schema and related frontend data types.
- The frontend and backend API contract was not defined early enough, which resulted in changes to field names, data types, and folder structure.
- Frontend and backend integration required more time than originally planned, and some frontend features still relied on mock data.
- The H2 in-memory database loses its data whenever the backend application stops, which was not initially expected.

---

## Things to Keep

- Continue actively participating in team meetings and project discussions.
- Maintain good communication and cooperation between team members.
- Continue supporting each other and working together when problems arise.


## Things to Change

- Team members should provide real-time progress updates instead of waiting until meetings.
- Assigned tasks should be started earlier to avoid completing too much work near the end of the Sprint.
- GitHub Projects, team chats, or another shared platform should be used to record progress and blockers.
- Code should be committed promptly after a feature is completed so other members can review it and continue their work.
- Sufficient testing time should be allocated to each feature, and testing should be included in the Definition of Done.
